1
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:14,860
So he slept there, much-enduring noble Odysseus,
overcome with sleep and weariness. But Athena

2
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:22,120
went her way to the land and city of the Phaeacian men,
who once, in years past, lived in spacious Hypereia,

3
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:29,700
near the Cyclopes, men of overweening pride,
who kept raiding them, and were the stronger in force.

4
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:34,300
From there godlike Nausithous
had uprooted them and led them,

5
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:41,560
and settled them in Scheria, far from men who eat bread,
and drove a wall around the city, and built houses,

6
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:45,880
and made temples for the gods,
and divided out the plowlands.

7
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:50,540
But he long since had gone down to Hades, broken by fate,

8
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:54,620
and now Alcinous ruled, his counsels known from the gods.

9
00:00:54,620 --> 00:01:01,200
To his house went the goddess, gray-eyed Athena,
devising the homecoming of great-hearted Odysseus.

10
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:08,000
She went into the richly-worked chamber where a girl
lay sleeping, like the immortals in build and beauty:

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,920
Nausicaa, daughter of great-hearted Alcinous;
and by her two handmaids, with beauty from the Graces,

12
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,920
one at each doorpost; and the shining doors were shut.

13
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:22,920
Like a breath of wind she swept to the girl's
bed and stood above her head, and spoke to her,

14
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:29,200
in the likeness of the daughter of Dymas, famed for
ships, a girl her own age, and dear to her heart.

15
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,220
In her likeness gray-eyed Athena said:

16
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,200
"Nausicaa, how did your mother bear so careless a girl?

17
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,980
Your shining clothes lie there uncared-for — and your
wedding is near, when you must wear beautiful things

18
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:44,470
yourself, and provide them for
those who lead you in procession:

19
00:01:44,470 --> 00:01:47,860
it is from things like these that a good name goes up

20
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:51,760
among the people, and a father
and honored mother are glad.

21
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:54,820
Come, let us go washing at the showing of dawn:

22
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,800
I will come with you and work
beside you, so you may be ready

23
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,480
the sooner — for truly not much longer will you be unwed:

24
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:07,360
already the best men through all the land of the
Phaeacians are wooing you — and your own line is theirs.

25
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:13,340
Come then: press your famous father, before the dawn,
to make ready the mules and a wagon to carry

26
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:16,600
the sashes and the robes and the shining coverlets.

27
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:19,620
And for yourself, too,
it is far more fitting so than going

28
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:23,660
on foot: the washing-pools are a long way from the city."

29
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:28,940
So speaking, gray-eyed Athena went away to Olympus,
where they say the seat of the gods stands fast

30
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:35,340
forever: not shaken by winds, never soaked by rain,
nor does snow come near it, but clear sky

31
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:39,120
spreads cloudless there,
and a white radiance runs over it;

32
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:42,520
in that place the blessed gods
take pleasure all their days.

33
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,460
There the gray-eyed one went,
when she had counseled the girl.

34
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:51,820
At once Dawn came on her fair throne, and woke Nausicaa
of the lovely robe; and she wondered at her dream

35
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:57,080
and went through the house to tell her parents,
her dear father and mother. She found them within:

36
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:02,740
her mother sat at the hearth with her attendant women,
spinning yarn dyed sea-purple; her father she met

37
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:09,020
at the door, on his way out to join the famous kings
in council, where the lordly Phaeacians called him.

38
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:11,780
She stood close to her dear father and said:

39
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:14,040
"Papa dear, won't you make ready a wagon for me,

40
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:17,220
the high one with the good wheels,
so I can take the fine clothes

41
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:20,760
to the river for washing, that lie about me dirty?

42
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:24,000
And for you too it is only proper,
when you sit among the first men

43
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,760
debating counsels, to have clean clothing on your body.

44
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:33,620
And five dear sons have been born to you in these halls —
two married, and three flourishing bachelors —

45
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:35,940
and they are always wanting fresh-washed clothes to wear

46
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,340
when they go to the dancing. All this is on my mind."

47
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:45,720
So she spoke: she was shy of naming ripe marriage
to her dear father. But he saw it all, and answered:

48
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,160
"I do not grudge you the mules, child, or anything else.

49
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,120
Go: the slaves will make ready a wagon for you,

50
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,380
the high one with the good wheels,
fitted with its top-rack."

51
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,680
So saying he called to the slaves, and they obeyed.

52
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:03,500
Outside, they made ready the smooth-running mule
wagon and led the mules under and yoked them to it;

53
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,740
and the girl brought the shining clothing from
her room and laid it in the polished wagon,

54
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:12,920
and her mother packed a box with heartening food
of every kind — put in delicacies, and poured wine

55
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:16,320
in a goatskin bag; and the girl climbed up on the wagon.

56
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:19,280
And she gave her soft olive oil in a golden flask,

57
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:22,900
so she and her attendant women might
anoint themselves after bathing.

58
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,500
And she took up the whip and the glossy reins

59
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,160
and whipped the mules to a run;
and there was a clatter of hooves,

60
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:32,460
and they strained on without slackening,
carrying the clothes and her —

61
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,220
not alone: her handmaids went along beside her.

62
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:37,480
And when they came to the beautiful stream of the river,

63
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:40,840
where the washing-pools never fail — plenty of clear water

64
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:44,560
welling up through and running over,
to clean the dirtiest things —

65
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:48,740
there they loosed the mules from under the
wagon and shooed them along the eddying river

66
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:52,240
to graze the honey-sweet water-grass;
and they lifted the clothes

67
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:55,600
from the wagon in armfuls and
carried them into the dark water,

68
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:59,060
and trod them briskly in the trenches, racing one another.

69
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:01,380
And when they had washed and rinsed away all the dirt,

70
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:04,940
they spread everything out in
rows along the shingle of the sea,

71
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:08,180
where the waves washed the pebbles
cleanest against the shore.

72
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:11,560
And when they had bathed and
rubbed themselves richly with oil,

73
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:17,080
they took their meal by the banks of the river and
waited for the clothes to dry in the shining of the sun.

74
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:19,960
And when she and her slave-women
had had their joy of food,

75
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:25,480
they threw off their head-veils and played at ball,
and white-armed Nausicaa led them in the singing.

76
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:30,960
As Artemis the arrow-pourer goes down the
mountains, down long Taygetus or Erymanthus,

77
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:36,580
delighting in the boars and the running deer,
and with her the nymphs, daughters of aegis-bearing Zeus,

78
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:42,700
play across the wild country, and Leto's heart is glad,
for she carries her head and brow above them all

79
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:45,160
and is known at once, though all of them are beautiful:

80
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,880
so the unwed girl outshone her handmaids.

81
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:53,560
But when she was about to fold the beautiful clothes,
yoke the mules, and turn for home again,

82
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:57,420
then the goddess thought of one
thing more, gray-eyed Athena:

83
00:05:57,420 --> 00:06:00,840
that Odysseus should wake, and see the lovely-eyed girl,

84
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:04,140
and she should lead him to the city of the Phaeacian men.

85
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:07,280
So the princess tossed the ball to one of her handmaids;

86
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:14,040
the handmaid she missed, and it fell in a deep eddy,
and they shrieked aloud. And noble Odysseus woke,

87
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:17,180
and sitting up he turned it over in his mind and heart:

88
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:20,540
"Ah me — whose country have I come to now? What men's?

89
00:06:20,540 --> 00:06:26,240
Are they violent, savage, without justice,
or kind to strangers, with hearts that fear the gods?

90
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:30,080
A female crying rang around me just now — girls' voices:

91
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:35,140
nymphs, who hold the sheer peaks of the mountains and
the springs of rivers and the grassy water-meadows?

92
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:38,500
Or am I now, can it be, near men with human speech?

93
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:41,540
Come — I will test it for myself, and see."

94
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,040
So speaking noble Odysseus slipped out from the thicket,

95
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:47,620
and from the thick wood with
his heavy hand he broke a branch

96
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:51,620
in leaf, to hold about his body
and screen a man's privates.

97
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:57,500
And he went like a mountain-bred lion, sure of his strength,
who goes on through rain and wind, his two eyes

98
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:02,740
burning, and he comes down on the cattle or the
sheep or the wild deer, for his belly commands him

99
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,640
to make trial of the flocks —
even to enter the close-built fold:

100
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:12,900
so Odysseus was about to come among the fair-braided
girls, naked as he was: for need had come upon him.

101
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:17,120
And he loomed terrible before them,
crusted with the salt sea's brine,

102
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:21,380
and they scattered, one here,
one there, along the jutting sandspits.

103
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:23,880
Alone the daughter of Alcinous stayed: for Athena

104
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:27,460
set courage in her heart and took
the trembling from her limbs.

105
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:32,000
She stood and faced him, holding
her ground. And Odysseus weighed it,

106
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,640
whether to clasp the knees of the lovely-eyed girl and beg,
or standing apart as he was, with honeyed words,

107
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:41,300
to beg her to show him the city and give him clothing.

108
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,680
And as he weighed it, this seemed the better way:

109
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:49,220
to stand apart and beg with honeyed words,
lest the girl's heart take anger if he touched her knees.

110
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:52,740
At once he spoke — a honeyed speech, and a shrewd one:

111
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:55,980
"I am at your knees, queen — goddess or mortal, are you?

112
00:07:55,980 --> 00:08:01,020
If you are one of the gods who hold wide heaven,
then it is to Artemis, daughter of great Zeus,

113
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:04,480
that I liken you nearest —
in beauty, in stature, in bearing.

114
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:07,500
But if you are one of the mortals who live on the earth,

115
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:10,260
three times blessed your father and your honored mother,

116
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:12,620
three times blessed your brothers! Surely their hearts

117
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:15,540
are warmed through with gladness always because of you,

118
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:18,720
watching such a young shoot go stepping into the dance.

119
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:21,020
But blessed at heart above all others is that man

120
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:24,540
who weighs down the scales of the
bride-gifts and leads you home.

121
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:30,560
For my eyes have never seen a mortal like you,
not man, not woman. Awe takes me, looking at you.

122
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:37,380
In Delos once, beside Apollo's altar, I saw such a thing:
the young shoot of a date-palm springing up —

123
00:08:37,380 --> 00:08:42,900
for I came there too, and a great host followed me,
on that road where my bitter cares were to be —

124
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,720
and just as, seeing that, I stood amazed at heart

125
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:50,040
a long time, since no such stem
had ever risen from the earth,

126
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:56,820
so, lady, I wonder at you, amazed, and I am terribly
afraid to touch your knees. But hard grief is upon me.

127
00:08:56,820 --> 00:09:00,700
Yesterday, on the twentieth day,
I escaped the wine-dark sea:

128
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:04,100
all that time the waves carried me,
and the driving storms,

129
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:08,280
away from the island Ogygia.
And now some power has cast me here —

130
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:11,880
to suffer some new evil in this place too,
no doubt: for I do not think

131
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:15,920
the evil will stop; the gods will
accomplish plenty more first.

132
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:21,160
But pity me, queen. After many hard labors it
is to you first I have come; I know not one

133
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:24,160
of the others whose city and land this is.

134
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:27,440
Show me the town, and give me a rag to throw around me —

135
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:31,320
if perhaps you had some wrapping-cloth
for the linen when you came.

136
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:34,540
And may the gods grant you all your heart desires:

137
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:38,700
a husband and a house — and may they add like-mindedness,

138
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:43,040
the good kind. For nothing is
stronger or better than that:

139
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:49,340
when two who think alike keep house together,
a man and his wife — much grief to their ill-wishers,

140
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:53,440
joy to their friends; and they themselves know it best."

141
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:55,860
Then white-armed Nausicaa answered him:

142
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:59,460
"Stranger — since you seem
neither base nor witless a man —

143
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:06,260
it is Olympian Zeus himself who deals out fortune to
mankind, to good and bad, to each one as he wills;

144
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:11,180
and to you, I suppose, he gave this lot,
and you must bear it, come what may.

145
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:13,940
But now, since you have come to our city and our land,

146
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:19,560
you shall not want for clothing, nor for anything
else that is owed a hard-worn suppliant when he comes.

147
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:23,360
I will show you the town,
and tell you the name of the people.

148
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:27,780
The Phaeacians hold this city and this land,
and I am the daughter of great-hearted Alcinous,

149
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:31,480
on whom the strength and power of the Phaeacians hang."

150
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:34,840
She spoke, and called to her fair-braided handmaids:

151
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:38,320
"Stand still, you girls! Where are
you running at the sight of a man?

152
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:40,900
You cannot think he is one of an enemy people?

153
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:45,480
There is no living mortal, and there will be none,
who could come bearing war into the land

154
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:48,680
of the Phaeacian men. For we are
very dear to the deathless ones,

155
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:55,340
and we live apart, in the much-surging sea,
at the world's edge, and no other mortals mix with us.

156
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:58,600
No — this is some poor wanderer come here astray,

157
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:02,540
whom we must care for now.
From Zeus they come, all of them,

158
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:06,520
strangers and beggars; and a gift, though small, is dear.

159
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:09,240
Come, girls: give the stranger food and drink,

160
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:12,500
and bathe him in the river,
where there is shelter from the wind."

161
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,840
So she spoke; and they stood, and called to one another,

162
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:20,200
and they set Odysseus down in the
sheltered place, as she commanded —

163
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:25,860
Nausicaa, daughter of great-hearted Alcinous —
and laid a mantle and a tunic by him for clothing,

164
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:31,760
and gave him soft olive oil in the golden flask,
and urged him to bathe in the streams of the river.

165
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:34,680
Then noble Odysseus spoke among the handmaids:

166
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:38,200
"Stand off there, girls, at a distance, so that I myself

167
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:41,080
may wash the brine from my shoulders, and rub myself

168
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:44,800
with oil: it is long indeed since oil came near my skin.

169
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:50,400
But I will not bathe in front of you. I would be
ashamed to stand naked among fair-braided girls."

170
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:53,720
So he spoke; and they went apart, and told the girl.

171
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:56,660
And noble Odysseus washed his body in the river,

172
00:11:56,660 --> 00:12:00,000
sluicing off the brine that caked
his back and broad shoulders,

173
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,220
and scrubbed from his head the
salt-scurf of the barren sea.

174
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:06,600
And when he had washed all over,
and rubbed himself with oil,

175
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:12,320
and put on the clothes the unwed girl had given him,
then Athena, born of Zeus, made him

176
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:15,580
taller to look at, and broader, and down from his head

177
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:19,580
she made the locks flow in curls,
like the hyacinth flower.

178
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:24,900
As when a man lays gold over silver — a craftsman
whom Hephaestus and Pallas Athena have taught

179
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:32,280
art of every kind, and he finishes works full of grace —
so she poured grace over his head and shoulders.

180
00:12:32,280 --> 00:12:35,160
Then he went apart and sat on the shingle of the sea,

181
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:40,900
shining with beauty and grace. And the girl gazed at him,
and spoke among her fair-braided handmaids:

182
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:44,460
"Listen, my white-armed girls, while I say something.

183
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:50,000
Not against the will of all the gods who hold Olympus
does this man come among the godlike Phaeacians.

184
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:56,440
Before, I confess, he seemed to me a sorry sight;
now he is like the gods who hold wide heaven.

185
00:12:56,440 --> 00:13:01,400
If only such a man might be called my husband,
living here — if only it pleased him to stay!

186
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:04,700
But come, girls: give the stranger food and drink."

187
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:09,800
So she spoke, and they listened well and obeyed,
and set food and drink beside Odysseus.

188
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:18,280
And he drank and ate, much-enduring noble Odysseus,
ravenously: it was long since he had tasted food.

189
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:22,300
But white-armed Nausicaa thought of one thing more:

190
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:25,640
she folded the clothes and stowed
them in the beautiful wagon,

191
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:29,560
and yoked the strong-hoofed mules, and mounted herself,

192
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:33,520
and called Odysseus on, and spoke a word, and named him:

193
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:37,140
"Up now, stranger, and we go
to the city, so I may send you

194
00:13:37,140 --> 00:13:43,220
to the house of my wise father — where, I promise you,
you will meet the best men of all the Phaeacians.

195
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:47,040
Only do exactly this —
you do not seem to me short of sense.

196
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:52,720
While we are passing the fields and the works of men,
walk with the handmaids, behind the mules and wagon,

197
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:54,980
briskly; I will lead the way.

198
00:13:54,980 --> 00:13:58,280
But when we are about to enter
the city — a high wall rings it,

199
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:01,020
and a beautiful harbor lies on either side of the town,

200
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:04,200
with a narrow causeway in;
and the curved ships are drawn up

201
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:07,360
along the road, for every man has his own slip there;

202
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:10,980
and there is the gathering-place
around Poseidon's fine precinct,

203
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:16,840
fitted with quarried stones set deep in the earth;
and there men see to the tackle of their black ships,

204
00:14:16,840 --> 00:14:20,140
the cables and the sails, and fine down their oars —

205
00:14:20,140 --> 00:14:23,040
for the Phaeacians care nothing for the bow or the quiver,

206
00:14:23,040 --> 00:14:26,900
but for masts, and ships' oars,
and the trim ships themselves,

207
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:30,080
in which they delight as they cross the gray salt sea —

208
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:33,420
it is their ugly talk I shrink from:
someone behind my back

209
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:39,700
There are insolent men enough in the town;
and one of the meaner sort, meeting us, might say:

210
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:42,600
'Who is this following Nausicaa — this tall handsome

211
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:46,540
stranger? Where did she find him?
A husband for her, no doubt!

212
00:14:46,540 --> 00:14:52,340
Some castaway she has taken up, off his ship,
a man from far parts — since nobody lives near us;

213
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:58,740
or some god, long prayed-for, come down to her out of
heaven in answer, and hers he will be all her days!

214
00:14:58,740 --> 00:15:01,580
Better so, if she has gone out and found herself a husband

215
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:05,040
from elsewhere — since she
scorns her own, here in the land,

216
00:15:05,040 --> 00:15:07,740
the Phaeacians, though many fine men are wooing her!'

217
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:10,840
So they will say, and it would be a reproach against me.

218
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:14,020
And I would blame another girl who did such things myself:

219
00:15:14,020 --> 00:15:16,760
who, against the will of her
dear father and mother living,

220
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:19,800
kept company with men before the day of open marriage.

221
00:15:19,800 --> 00:15:25,920
So, stranger, mark my word quickly, and you will
win escort and homecoming from my father soonest.

222
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:28,900
You will find a shining grove of Athena near the road —

223
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:32,920
poplars; a spring wells up in it; a meadow lies around it.

224
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:38,620
There is my father's park there, and his flourishing orchard,
as far from the city as a man's shout carries.

225
00:15:38,620 --> 00:15:43,820
Sit down there and wait a while, until we have
entered the town and reached my father's house.

226
00:15:43,820 --> 00:15:48,600
But when you judge that we have reached the house,
then go into the city of the Phaeacians, and ask

227
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,680
for the house of my father, great-hearted Alcinous.

228
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:57,460
It is easily known: a little child could lead you —
a baby; for the houses of the Phaeacians are not built

229
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:00,880
anything like it — such is the house of the hero

230
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:03,380
Alcinous. And once the buildings
and the court have hidden you,

231
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:06,320
go quickly straight through the great hall, until you come

232
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:10,040
to my mother. She sits at the
hearth in the light of the fire,

233
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:13,360
spinning yarn dyed sea-purple — a wonder to see —

234
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:17,000
leaning against a pillar,
and her slave-women sit behind her.

235
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:22,580
And there my father's chair is tilted close to hers,
where he sits and drinks his wine like an immortal.

236
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:27,860
Pass him by, and throw your arms around the knees of
my mother — if you would see your day of homecoming

237
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:31,940
with rejoicing, and quickly,
though you come from very far.

238
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:34,760
If she once takes kindly thought for you in her heart,

239
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:40,360
then there is hope you will see your own people,
and come to your well-built house and your own fatherland."

240
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:45,720
So speaking she touched the mules with the shining whip,
and they quickly left the flowing river behind.

241
00:16:45,720 --> 00:16:49,260
Well they trotted, well they wheeled their legs along,

242
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:53,120
and she drove with care,
so that the walkers could keep pace —

243
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:57,360
the handmaids and Odysseus —
and used the lash with judgment.

244
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:04,920
And the sun went down, and they came to the famous
grove sacred to Athena. There noble Odysseus sat down,

245
00:17:04,920 --> 00:17:08,040
and at once he prayed to the daughter of great Zeus:

246
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:12,200
"Hear me, child of aegis-bearing Zeus, Unwearied One.

247
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:15,080
Hear me now at last, since before you never heard me

248
00:17:15,080 --> 00:17:18,740
when I was broken — when the famous earth-shaker broke me.

249
00:17:18,740 --> 00:17:22,840
Grant that I come among the
Phaeacians pitied, and a friend."

250
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:26,680
So he spoke in prayer, and Pallas Athena heard him.

251
00:17:26,680 --> 00:17:30,460
But she did not yet show herself
to him face to face: she stood

252
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:33,520
in awe of her father's brother, who raged on furiously

253
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:38,090
against godlike Odysseus,
until the day he reached his own land.
